= A
{parent=2010}
{scope}

= 1
{parent=2010/A}
{scope}

= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $\mathbf x(\mathbf X,t)$ be the particle <flow map> from the fixed reference volume $V_0$ to the <material volume>, and let $J=\det(\partial\mathbf x/\partial\mathbf X)$. Change variables and differentiate on the fixed domain:
$$
\frac d{dt}\int_{V(t)}f\,dV
=\int_{V_0}\left(\frac{Df}{Dt}J+f\frac{DJ}{Dt}\right)dV_0.
$$
Use the two permitted identities, $DJ/Dt=J\nabla\cdot\mathbf u$ and $Df/Dt=f_t+\mathbf u\cdot\nabla f$. Changing back gives
$$
\boxed{\frac d{dt}\int_{V(t)}f\,dV
=\int_{V(t)}\left(f_t+\mathbf u\cdot\nabla f+f\nabla\cdot\mathbf u\right)dV
=\int_{V(t)}\left(f_t+\nabla\cdot(f\mathbf u)\right)dV.}
$$
This is the <Reynolds transport theorem>: both the value carried by each particle and the changing volume element contribute.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <traction> $\mathbf t(\mathbf n)$ is force per unit area across an oriented surface with unit normal $\mathbf n$. The stress component $\sigma_{ij}$ is its $i$th component on a surface normal to the $j$th coordinate axis. <Cauchy's stress theorem> states that this dependence is linear:
$$
\boxed{t_i(\mathbf n)=\sigma_{ij}n_j.}
$$
For the boundary of a <material volume>, take the outward normal and the <traction> exerted by the surrounding fluid. Newton's law and the <divergence theorem> give
$$
\frac d{dt}\int_{V(t)}\rho u_i\,dV
=\int_{\partial V(t)}\sigma_{ij}n_j\,dS
=\int_{V(t)}\partial_j\sigma_{ij}\,dV.
$$
Constant <density> and <incompressibility> make the transport-theorem integrand on the left $\rho Du_i/Dt$. The volume is arbitrary, so
$$
\boxed{\rho\frac{Du_i}{Dt}=\partial_j\sigma_{ij}.}
$$
Substitute the Newtonian constitutive law. With constant viscosity,
$$
\partial_j\sigma_{ij}=-\partial_i p+\mu\partial_j\partial_j u_i
+\mu\partial_i(\partial_j u_j)=-\partial_i p+\mu\nabla^2u_i.
$$
Hence
$$
\boxed{\rho(\mathbf u_t+(\mathbf u\cdot\nabla)\mathbf u)
=-\nabla p+\mu\nabla^2\mathbf u.}
$$

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For a steady unidirectional field $\mathbf u=w\mathbf k$, <incompressibility> gives $\partial_z w=0$. Thus $w=w(x,y)$ and the convective acceleration vanishes. The transverse momentum equations give $p_x=p_y=0$, so $p=p(z)$. The axial equation is
$$
\mu\Delta_\perp w=p'(z).
$$
Its left side is independent of $z$ and its right side independent of $x,y$, so both equal a constant $G$. No slip against the translating wall gives $w=U$ on the boundary. Thus the asserted Poisson problem follows.

For the circular pipe, the axisymmetric solution is
$$
\boxed{w(r)=U+\frac G{4\mu}(r^2-R^2).}
$$
It satisfies the boundary value and $\Delta_\perp r^2=4$. Integrating over the cross-section gives
$$
Q=2\pi\int_0^Rw(r)r\,dr=\pi R^2U-\frac{\pi R^4G}{8\mu}.
$$
Therefore the laboratory-frame <volume flux> vanishes precisely when
$$
\boxed{U=\frac{R^2}{8\mu}\frac{dp}{dz}.}
$$
For a <pressure> decrease in the positive axial direction, this requires upstream wall translation.

= 2
{parent=2010/A}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Balance streamwise convection of order one with transverse viscous diffusion $Re^{-1}\partial_y^2u$. This requires a thickness $Re^{-1/2}$. Continuity then makes the normal <velocity> of that same small order. Set
$$
\boxed{Y=Re^{1/2}y,\qquad V=Re^{1/2}v.}
$$
The normal momentum equation makes the leading <pressure> independent of $Y$. It equals the outer <pressure>, whose inviscid tangential momentum balance gives $-p_x=U_sU_s'$. The leading tangential equation and continuity are consequently
$$
\boxed{uu_x+Vu_Y=U_sU_s'+u_{YY},\qquad u_x+V_Y=0.}
$$
The <divergence>-free field $(u,V)$ has a <streamfunction> with $u=\Psi_Y$, $V=-\Psi_x$. Substituting yields
$$
\boxed{\Psi_Y\Psi_{xY}-\Psi_x\Psi_{YY}=U_sU_s'+\Psi_{YYY}.}
$$
The stationary impermeable wall has $u=V=0$, so $\Psi_Y(x,0)=0$ and $\Psi(x,0)$ is constant, chosen to be zero. Matching to the outer slip <velocity> gives $\Psi_Y(x,\infty)=U_s(x)$.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For the proposed form,
$$
u=x^{\alpha-\beta}f',\quad
V=-x^{\alpha-1}(\alpha f-\beta\eta f'),\quad
u_Y=x^{\alpha-2\beta}f''.
$$
Matching the outer <velocity> requires $\alpha-\beta=m$. The convective and outer-<pressure> terms have order $x^{2m-1}$, while $u_{YY}$ has order $x^{\alpha-3\beta}$. Equality of exponents gives
$$
\boxed{\alpha=\frac{1+m}{2},\qquad\beta=\frac{1-m}{2}.}
$$
The two terms containing $\eta f'f''$ cancel in $uu_x+Vu_Y$, leaving $x^{2m-1}[m(f')^2-\alpha ff'']$. Since $U_sU_s'=mA^2x^{2m-1}$, the reduced equation is
$$
\boxed{f^{(3)}+\frac{1+m}{2}ff''+m\{A^2-(f')^2\}=0.}
$$
The wall and matching conditions become $\boxed{f(0)=f'(0)=0,\ f'(\infty)=A}$. This establishes a consistent similarity reduction; existence of an actual profile is a separate boundary-value question for a particular choice of parameters.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

At these parameters the equation becomes $g''+g^2-4=0$, where $g=f'$. Multiply by $g'$ and integrate:
$$
\frac12(g')^2+\frac13g^3-4g=C.
$$
Outer matching gives $g\to-2$ and $g'\to0$, so $C=16/3$. No slip gives $g(0)=0$. At that point the <first integral> fixes $g'(0)=\pm4\sqrt{2/3}$, so there are exactly two smooth initial-value solutions of the second-order equation satisfying the matching condition. Put $\eta_0=\operatorname{arctanh}\sqrt{2/3}$. Translation invariance of the supplied profile gives them explicitly:
$$
\boxed{g_-(\eta)=4-6\tanh^2(\eta+\eta_0),\qquad
 g_+(\eta)=4-6\tanh^2(\eta-\eta_0).}
$$
The first decreases monotonically from zero to $-2$. The second first rises to $4$ at $\eta_0$, crosses zero again at $2\eta_0$, and then tends to $-2$. Integrating either profile from zero recovers $f$ with $f(0)=0$.

\Image[2010-boundary-layer-branches.png]
{title=Two translated similarity profiles: monotone outer-flow matching and a branch with an internal reversed-flow region}

One would **not expect both branches to be equally stable**. The rising branch contains flow opposite to its negative outer stream and interior <inflection points>, typical ingredients for <shear instability>. The monotone branch has $g''=4-g^2>0$ at finite positive $\eta$ and no interior <inflection point>, so the classical inviscid inflection-point mechanism is absent there. This motivates expecting the reversed-flow branch to be unstable and the monotone branch to be the stable candidate. A definitive viscous temporal-stability result requires perturbing the time-dependent equations; the steady <first integral> alone is not a stability proof.

= 3
{parent=2010/A}
{scope}

= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Introduce $\mathcal R=\rho UL/\mu$. Balancing the streamwise <pressure gradient> $\Pi/L$ with cross-layer viscosity $\mu U/(\delta^2L^2)$ gives
$$
\boxed{\Pi=\frac{\mu U}{\delta^2L}.}
$$
After division by that viscous scale, the streamwise momentum equation is
$$
\delta^2\mathcal R\bigl(\widehat u_{\widehat t}+\widehat u\widehat u_{\widehat x}
+\widehat w\widehat u_{\widehat z}\bigr)
=-\widehat p_{\widehat x}+\widehat u_{\widehat z\widehat z}
+\delta^2\widehat u_{\widehat x\widehat x}+S,
\quad S=\frac{\delta^2\rho gL^2}{\mu U}.
$$
The transverse equation, divided by its <pressure>-<gradient> scale, is
$$
\delta^4\mathcal R\bigl(\widehat w_{\widehat t}+\widehat u\widehat w_{\widehat x}
+\widehat w\widehat w_{\widehat z}\bigr)
=-\widehat p_{\widehat z}+\delta^2\widehat w_{\widehat z\widehat z}
+\delta^4\widehat w_{\widehat x\widehat x}.
$$
Continuity scales exactly to $\widehat u_{\widehat x}+\widehat w_{\widehat z}=0$. With $\delta\ll1$, $\delta^2\mathcal R\ll1$ and $S=O(1)$, the leading equations are
$$
\boxed{0=-\widehat p_{\widehat x}+\widehat u_{\widehat z\widehat z}+S,
\qquad\widehat p_{\widehat z}=0,
\qquad\widehat u_{\widehat x}+\widehat w_{\widehat z}=0.}
$$

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

No slip and impermeability give dimensional $u=-U$, $w=0$ on the upward-moving wall, so $\boxed{\widehat u=-1,\ \widehat w=0}$ there. For the interface normal, $h_x=\delta\widehat h_{\widehat x}$, and hence
$$
\mathbf n=(\delta\widehat h_{\widehat x},-1)+O(\delta^2)
$$
in its $x,z$ components. The relevant stresses scale as
$$
\sigma_{xx}=-\Pi\widehat p+O(\mu U/L),\qquad
\sigma_{xz}=\frac{\mu U}{\delta L}\widehat u_{\widehat z}+O(\delta\mu U/L),
\qquad\sigma_{zz}=-\Pi\widehat p+O(\mu U/L).
$$
Multiplying by the inward normal gives the leading <traction>
$$
\boxed{\mathbf t\sim-\frac{\mu U}{\delta L}
(\widehat p\widehat h_{\widehat x}+\widehat u_{\widehat z})\mathbf i
+\frac{\mu U}{\delta^2L}\widehat p\mathbf k.}
$$
Its leading normal component vanishes only if $\widehat p=0$ at the interface; the tangential component then requires $\widehat u_{\widehat z}=0$.

The <free surface> is material. Applying the <material derivative> to $z-h(x,t)=0$ gives $w=h_t+uh_x$, which in the scaled variables becomes
$$
\boxed{\widehat w=\widehat h_{\widehat t}+\widehat u\widehat h_{\widehat x},\qquad
\widehat p=0,\qquad\widehat u_{\widehat z}=0
\quad\text{at }\widehat z=\widehat h.}
$$

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Suppress hats for this calculation. Transverse <pressure> uniformity and zero surface <pressure> give $p=0$ throughout the film. Thus $u_{zz}=-S$. Integrating twice, using $u_z(h)=0$ and $u(0)=-1$, gives
$$
\boxed{u(z)=-1+S(hz-z^2/2).}
$$
The flux is $q=\int_0^h u\,dz=-h+Sh^3/3$. Integrate continuity across the layer and apply the wall and kinematic conditions to obtain $h_t+q_x=0$. Restoring hats yields
$$
\boxed{\widehat h_{\widehat t}+(S\widehat h^2-1)\widehat h_{\widehat x}=0.}
$$
The cubic flux contains downward <gravity>-driven transport and upward wall entrainment.

