= 4
{parent=2010/B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <material derivative> $\boxed{D/Dt=\partial_t+\mathbf u\cdot\nabla}$ measures change while following a moving gas parcel. The four equations respectively express **<mass conservation>**, **<momentum conservation>**, **internal-energy balance**, and the **<ideal gas law>**. The first accounts for <density> change under expansion or compression. The second is Newton's law with <pressure> as the only force. In the third the specific internal energy is $c_vT$ and <pressure> work $-p\nabla\cdot\mathbf u$ heats compression and cools expansion. The fourth relates thermodynamic <pressure>, <density> and temperature; $R$ is the gas constant per unit mass.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Continuity gives $D\log\rho/Dt=-\nabla\cdot\mathbf u$. Using $p=\rho RT$, the temperature equation gives $D\log T/Dt=-(R/c_v)\nabla\cdot\mathbf u$. Therefore
$$
\frac{D\log p}{Dt}=\frac{D\log\rho}{Dt}+\frac{D\log T}{Dt}
=-\gamma\nabla\cdot\mathbf u,\qquad\gamma=1+R/c_v.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{\frac{DS}{Dt}=c_v\left(\frac{D\log p}{Dt}-\gamma\frac{D\log\rho}{Dt}\right)=0.}
$$
Each smooth parcel evolves isentropically under these adiabatic inviscid equations; the argument does not cover <entropy>-producing shocks.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Conservation of the parcel's <entropy> makes $p/\rho^\gamma$ constant along its trajectory. Its initial value is $p_0/\rho_0^\gamma$, so
$$
\boxed{p=p_0\left(\frac\rho{\rho_0}\right)^\gamma.}
$$
This is a relation following the specified parcel; parcels with different initial <entropy> can have different constants.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

<Linearization> about the uniform resting state gives
$$
\rho'_t+\rho_0\nabla\cdot\mathbf u'=0,\qquad
\rho_0\mathbf u'_t=-\nabla p',\qquad
p'_t=-\gamma p_0\nabla\cdot\mathbf u'.
$$
For irrotational motion write $\mathbf u'=\nabla\phi$. The momentum equation integrates to $p'=-\rho_0\phi_t$ after choosing the time-dependent potential gauge. Substituting into the <pressure> equation yields
$$
\boxed{\phi_{tt}=c_0^2\nabla^2\phi,\qquad
c_0=\sqrt{\gamma p_0/\rho_0}.}
$$
The constant is the adiabatic <sound speed>, also given by $(\partial p/\partial\rho)_S^{1/2}$ at the base state.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Rigid impermeable walls require zero normal fluid <velocity>, so the potential satisfies a homogeneous Neumann condition. In the cylinder,
$$
\boxed{\phi_r(a,\theta,z,t)=0,\qquad
\phi_z(r,\theta,0,t)=\phi_z(r,\theta,h,t)=0.}
$$
It is also single-valued and periodic in $\theta$, and regular on the axis. No tangential <no-slip boundary condition> is imposed on this inviscid acoustic potential.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Use $\phi=F(r)\Theta(\theta)Z(z)e^{-i\omega t}$. Periodicity gives $\Theta=\cos m\theta$ or $\sin m\theta$, $m\geq0$, with only the cosine branch for $m=0$. The end-wall conditions give $Z=\cos(\ell\pi z/h)$, $\ell\geq0$. With radial separation constant $\kappa^2$, the remaining equation is
$$
F''+\frac1rF'+\left(\kappa^2-\frac{m^2}{r^2}\right)F=0,
\qquad\omega^2=c_0^2\left(\kappa^2+\frac{\ell^2\pi^2}{h^2}\right).
$$
For $\kappa>0$, boundedness on the axis selects $F=J_m(\kappa r)$. The cylindrical wall then requires $J_m'(\kappa a)=0$. Thus the modes are real and imaginary parts of
$$
\boxed{J_m(k_{m,n}r/a)\{\cos m\theta,\sin m\theta\}
\cos(\ell\pi z/h)e^{-i\omega t},\qquad
\omega^2=c_0^2\left(\frac{k_{m,n}^2}{a^2}+\frac{\ell^2\pi^2}{h^2}\right).}
$$
Here take the positive derivative zeros and include the separate constant radial mode $m=0$, $k=0$. For zero radial separation constant, regularity gives $r^m$ when $m>0$, which fails the wall condition, and a constant for $m=0$. This avoids counting a formal derivative zero at the origin that would make $J_m(0\,r)$ identically zero. The completely constant mode has zero frequency and no <velocity>; the remaining modes are acoustic oscillations. The self-adjoint <Neumann Laplacian> has these separated modes as a complete system, so no additional nonzero natural frequencies occur.

= 5
{parent=2010/B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

<Incompressibility> and $\mathbf u=\nabla\phi$ give $\boxed{\nabla^2\phi=0}$. The permitted vector identity, with zero <vorticity>, gives $(\mathbf u\cdot\nabla)\mathbf u=\nabla(|\mathbf u|^2/2)$. Substituting into Euler's equation yields
$$
\nabla\left(\rho\phi_t+\frac\rho2|\nabla\phi|^2+p+\rho gz\right)=0.
$$
The bracket is spatially constant in a connected fluid region, so
$$
\boxed{\rho\phi_t+\frac\rho2|\nabla\phi|^2+p+\rho gz=F(t).}
$$
Adding a function of time to the potential changes only $F(t)$ and leaves the <velocity> unchanged.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Each perturbation potential is harmonic in its own half-space:
$$
\boxed{\widetilde\phi_{i,xx}+\widetilde\phi_{i,zz}=0.}
$$
Write $D_1=\partial_t+(U/2)\partial_x$ and $D_2=\partial_t-(U/2)\partial_x$. Linearizing the material-interface condition at $z=0$ gives
$$
\boxed{D_1\eta=\widetilde\phi_{1,z},\qquad
D_2\eta=\widetilde\phi_{2,z}.}
$$
<Bernoulli's equation> at the displaced interface gives $p_i'= -\rho_i(D_i\widetilde\phi_i+g\eta)$. Using the supplied <pressure> jump therefore yields
$$
\boxed{\rho_1D_1\widetilde\phi_1-\rho_2D_2\widetilde\phi_2
=(\rho_2-\rho_1)g\eta-\gamma\eta_{xx}\quad(z=0).}
$$
The potentials decay toward their respective infinities. The hydrostatic displacement terms are retained in this dynamic condition.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Put $q=|k|>0$ and $\omega_1=\omega-kU/2$, $\omega_2=\omega+kU/2$. Harmonicity and decay select
$$
\widetilde\phi_1=B_1e^{-qz}e^{i(kx-\omega t)},\qquad
\widetilde\phi_2=B_2e^{qz}e^{i(kx-\omega t)}.
$$
The kinematic conditions give $B_1=i\omega_1A/q$ and $B_2=-i\omega_2A/q$. Inserting them into the dynamic condition and cancelling the nonzero interface amplitude gives
$$
\rho_1\omega_1^2+\rho_2\omega_2^2
=q\{(\rho_2-\rho_1)g+\gamma k^2\}.
$$
Expansion proves
$$
\boxed{(\rho_1+\rho_2)\omega^2+(\rho_2-\rho_1)Uk\omega
+\frac14(\rho_1+\rho_2)U^2k^2
-\{(\rho_2-\rho_1)g+\gamma k^2\}|k|=0.}
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Let $\Sigma=\rho_1+\rho_2$, $\Delta=\rho_2-\rho_1>0$. Completing the square in the <dispersion relation> gives
$$
\left(\omega+\frac{\Delta Uk}{2\Sigma}\right)^2
=\frac{|k|(\Delta g+\gamma k^2)}\Sigma
-\frac{\rho_1\rho_2U^2k^2}{\Sigma^2}.
$$
The coefficients are real. There is an exponentially growing temporal mode exactly when the right side is negative, because one root then has positive imaginary part in $e^{-i\omega t}$. Rearrangement gives
$$
\boxed{|U|>U_*(k),\qquad
U_*(k)=\left[\frac{\Sigma}{\rho_1\rho_2}
\left(\frac{\Delta g}{|k|}+\gamma|k|\right)\right]^{1/2}.}
$$
Equality is the marginal threshold rather than <exponential growth>.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

For positive <surface tension> the threshold is even in $k$, tends to infinity as $k\to0$ and as $|k|\to\infty$, and has its two equal minima at
$$
\boxed{|k|=k_*=\sqrt{\Delta g/\gamma}.}
$$
Minimizing $\Delta g/q+\gamma q$ for $q>0$ gives
$$
\boxed{U_{\min}=\left[\frac{2\Sigma}{\rho_1\rho_2}\sqrt{\Delta g\gamma}\right]^{1/2}.}
$$
Thus the initial state has some exponentially growing mode exactly when $|U|>U_{\min}$. If $D=\rho_1\rho_2U^2/\Sigma$, the unstable band is
$$
\boxed{q_-<|k|<q_+,\qquad
q_\pm=\frac{D\pm\sqrt{D^2-4\gamma\Delta g}}{2\gamma}.}
$$
<Gravity> suppresses long wavelengths and <surface tension> suppresses short ones; intermediate wavelengths become unstable when the shear is large enough.

\Image[2010-capillary-shear-threshold.png]
{title=Even instability threshold with minima at the gravity-capillary balance; the sample shear destabilizes the shaded intermediate bands}

= 6
{parent=2010/B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For positive depth put $c=\sqrt{gh}$. The equations become
$$
u_t+uu_x+2cc_x=0,\qquad c_t+uc_x+\frac c2u_x=0.
$$
Adding or subtracting twice the second equation to the first gives
$$
\boxed{(\partial_t+(u\pm c)\partial_x)(u\pm2c)=0.}
$$
Thus the two <characteristic speeds> are $\boxed{dx/dt=u\pm c}$ and the corresponding conserved <Riemann invariants> are $\boxed{Q_\pm=u\pm2c}$. At a dry front $c=0$ the two speeds coincide; the positive-depth formulas are applied up to that limit.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Before a signal traveling left at speed $c_0$ can reach the wall, the portion $-a<x<-c_0t$ remains at rest with $c=c_0$, $h=h_0$. In the rarefaction fan, right-running characteristics bring the unchanged invariant $Q_+=2c_0$. The left-running family originates at the removed dam and has speed $u-c=x/t$. Solving these two relations gives
$$
u=\frac23(c_0+x/t),\qquad c=\frac13(2c_0-x/t).
$$
At the dry front $c=0$, so its speed and location are $2c_0$ and $2c_0t$, consistent with the stated constant-speed condition. Therefore, for $0<t<a/c_0$,
$$
\boxed{c(x,t)=\begin{cases}c_0,&-a<x<-c_0t,\\
(2c_0-x/t)/3,&-c_0t\leq x<2c_0t,
\end{cases}}
$$
$$
\boxed{h(x,t)=\begin{cases}h_0,&-a<x<-c_0t,\\
(2c_0-x/t)^2/(9g),&-c_0t\leq x<2c_0t.
\end{cases}}
$$
The <velocity> is zero in the untouched region and $2(c_0+x/t)/3$ in the fan. Ahead of the front there is no fluid, so $h=c=0$ and a fluid <velocity> need not be assigned. In particular the wall remains at $u=0,c=c_0$ until time $a/c_0$.

\Image[2010-dam-break-characteristics.png]
{title=Dam-break characteristics in units with a and c0 equal to one; the wall first affects the fan at time one}

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Inside the fan, $u+c=(4c_0+x/t)/3$. Hence a right-running characteristic satisfies
$$
\boxed{\frac{dx_+}{dt}-\frac{x_+}{3t}=\frac{4c_0}3.}
$$
Multiplication by the <integrating factor> $t^{-1/3}$ gives
$$
\frac d{dt}(t^{-1/3}x_+)=\frac{4c_0}{3}t^{-1/3}.
$$
Integration yields $x_+=2c_0t+Ct^{1/3}$. Requiring $x_+(\tau)=-c_0\tau$ determines $C=-3c_0\tau^{2/3}$, so
$$
\boxed{x_+(t)=2c_0t-3c_0\tau^{2/3}t^{1/3},\qquad t\geq\tau.}
$$
The invariant $Q_+=2c_0$ stays constant along it until it enters a region affected by new boundary data.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The head of the rarefaction reaches the wall at $(-a,t_0)$ with $t_0=a/c_0$. The right-running characteristic through that event is obtained by setting $\tau=t_0$ in the preceding formula:
$$
\boxed{x_b(t)=2c_0t-3a(c_0t/a)^{1/3}.}
$$
It bounds the region reached by the first wall-induced change in $Q_+$. To its right the characteristic information remains the original dam-break information, so
$$
\boxed{u=\frac23(c_0+x/t)\quad\text{for }x_b(t)\leq x<2c_0t.}
$$
Behind it, the impermeable wall must keep $u(-a,t)=0$. Continuing the original fan there would instead give $2(c_0-a/t)/3>0$ for $t>t_0$. Reflected characteristic data therefore change $Q_+$ and invalidate the simple fan formula. The shaded region in the characteristic diagram represents this changed <domain of dependence>; it includes $0<x<x_b(t)$ once that interval becomes nonempty. Its first positive extent occurs when $t/t_0>(3/2)^{3/2}$.
